#: ../../source/docs/common-accelerometer-calibration.rst:5
#: 6c25a942050d4a2485b7f25458e5c9c7
msgid "Accelerometer Calibration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-accelerometer-calibration.rst:9
#: ed06e058ddc946c3a5074daafea37105
msgid "This article shows how to perform basic accelerometer calibration (using *Mission Planner*). The accelerometers in the autopilot must be calibrated to correct for their bias offsets in all three axes, as well as any off-axis variations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-accelerometer-calibration.rst:14
#: 455f3b84b6b44fa88a09a4d49720e248
msgid "Accelerometer calibration is mandatory in ArduPilot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-accelerometer-calibration.rst:18
#: 7ff17343db614afaa2fe43d5d62b230a
msgid "Accelerometer calibration cannot be performed while the vehicle is armed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-accelerometer-calibration.rst:21
#: 532315b725a44729b5dc78a8772202d1
msgid "Calibration steps"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-accelerometer-calibration.rst:23
#: d6f26f75c2394a81a5e4d5b2bf772a97
msgid "If the board is mounted in a non-standard orientation (i.e. arrow is not pointing forward) then please ensure the :ref:`AHRS_ORIENTATION<AHRS_ORIENTATION>`  is properly set before doing the accelerometer calibration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-accelerometer-calibration.rst:25
#: d5560ba065ba432f851ede4914cbf3d3
msgid "For very large vehicles, this may be done on the bench, after the orientation is set for how it will be mounted in the vehicle and the calibration orientations are done as fit in the vehicle. Then be sure to use the **Calibrate Level** step in the following instructions once mounted."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-accelerometer-calibration.rst:27
#: 067ec0dcfe274d8996f5f60e637ed800
msgid "Under **Setup \\| Mandatory Hardware**, select **Accel Calibration** from the left-side menu."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-accelerometer-calibration.rst:32
#: cde2258fe2e943348e6ecef1b900e79e
msgid "Mission Planner: Calibrate Acceleration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-accelerometer-calibration.rst:34
#: 688deb7c4a6147cb851b8af14818a2eb
msgid "Click **Calibrate Accel** to start the full 3-axis calibration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-accelerometer-calibration.rst:36
#: 96ff8f5951884f0a8d16cee76226f16f
msgid "*Mission Planner* will prompt you to place the vehicle on each axis during the calibration. Press any key to indicate that the autopilot is in position and then proceed to the next orientation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-accelerometer-calibration.rst:38
#: f2571d9fc66244a8b25d5bba53aee443
msgid "The calibration positions are: level, right side, left side, nose down, nose up, and on its back."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-accelerometer-calibration.rst:43
#: 15edcf1eef534ff6b679b35b8489bb4b
msgid "Accelerometer Calibration Positions (Copter)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-accelerometer-calibration.rst:45
#: cfb39c4d3c9445af92681abc3ee7a34f
msgid "The vehicle must be kept still immediately after pressing the key for each step. This is more important than getting the angle exactly right, ie. left being 90deg to horizontal, etc. Except for the first \"LEVEL\", the positions can be within 20 degrees of being exact. **Being still in each position as you press the key is much more important.**.  Do not hold the vehicle when doing this step, find some way to pose the vehicle (e.g. lie on the ground or put it in a jig)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-accelerometer-calibration.rst:47
#: 6775199d9fa2464b91b4bdddd4b43aab
msgid "You should calibrate the board mounted in the vehicle if possible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-accelerometer-calibration.rst:48
#: 82c23606306142e5b664b440b7608a64
msgid "However, you may need to calibrate the board before it is mounted if the size/shape of the vehicle makes this difficult."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-accelerometer-calibration.rst:49
#: 9d2b2a10eb454be790567d7e92bc5e90
msgid "The level position is the most important to get right as this will be the attitude that your controller considers level while flying."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-accelerometer-calibration.rst:50
#: a3c549a4d11c473291c10b70c2108a98
msgid "You can recalibrate this Level position using Mission Planner after you have installed the autopilot and are ready to fly. Place the vehicle in its level flying attitude and use the **Calibrate Level** button."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-accelerometer-calibration.rst:52
#: bf68f10f37ce42cba30b40648eb80bde
msgid "this **Calibrate Level** operation can only correct up to a 10 degree difference between the initial calibration and the final position in the vehicle, and only corrects pitch and roll differences, not yaw."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-accelerometer-calibration.rst:55
#: 024da1d052c647f39cdedaf96d6a0cce
msgid "For planes, the \"level\" angle for pitch is important for steady altitude flight. See :ref:`tuning-cruise` for more details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-accelerometer-calibration.rst:57
#: 534c46194ffb498786ffd5815b6c052e
msgid "The :ref:`STAB_PITCH_DOWN<STAB_PITCH_DOWN>` parameter will add \"nose-down\" trim when the throttle stick is lowered in pilot throttle controlled and stabilized modes, such as FBWA, to prevent the autopilot from holding the nose up as the plane slows down and potentially causing a stall. This can be tested, at altitude, in FBWA mode by moving the throttle to idle and checking that there is sufficient airspeed in a turn to avoid stalling. Be prepared to recover from a stall! Increase the value of :ref:`STAB_PITCH_DOWN<STAB_PITCH_DOWN>`, if necessary."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-accelerometer-calibration.rst:59
#: 11531b68dffb45c6b915f8c6dfdd2081
msgid "For small planes or gliders, :ref:`STAB_PITCH_DOWN<STAB_PITCH_DOWN>` often needs to be set more than the default value of 2 degrees."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-accelerometer-calibration.rst:63
#: 1915482f19674906a5d28cb9507a30df
msgid "Proceed through the required positions, using the **Click when Done** button once each position is reached and held still."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-accelerometer-calibration.rst:65
#: 9922267fb7ec491ba4b93214789920e2
msgid "When you have completed the calibration process, Mission Planner will display \"Calibration Successful!\" as shown below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-accelerometer-calibration.rst:70
#: dd40fe11eb7f4dd88cac6b4905b67eee
msgid "Mission Planner: Calibration Successful"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-accelerometer-calibration.rst:72
#: d9e78fb9a6474e51b5d937b0e2611b69
msgid "If your autopilot has a built-in IMU heater, then it is recommended that the :ref:`common-imutempcal` also be done. Some autopilots have this calibration done at the factory in which case, this calibration need not be done."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-accelerometer-calibration.rst:75
#: 4d3637cddb9e4fa38bedf39eced582f6
msgid "Simple Calibration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-accelerometer-calibration.rst:77
#: 688bfbd14a794fd3be717919a539b7b5
msgid "Sometimes, for very large vehicles, it's not easy to do the full 3-axis calibration. In this case, the **Simple Accel Cal** can be done with the vehicle held still and in a level attitude. This only calibrates the main offsets of the accelerometers, not the minor off-axis variations, so it's not ideal in terms of optimal performance, but is sometimes an acceptable compromise."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-accelerometer-calibration.rst:79
#: a6099fc8cf7542f0956dc261d2993af8
msgid "This is **NOT** the same as the **Calibrate Level** function. To use that function, either a full 3-axis or simple calibration must be done first!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-accelerometer-calibration.rst:82
#: aa8d314045454a6485ce0d73fb6cc6ae
msgid "Video demonstration (Copter)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-accelerometer-calibration.rst:84
#: 463b2261267d403db767e76183a61245
msgid "Video demonstration of accelerometer calibration. This is for an older version of Copter/Mission Planner, but is useful as an example of how you might hold a Copter."
msgstr ""
